LESSON 5: USING THE DEPENDENCY ARRAY
AND ADDING VALUES THAT CONTROL SIDE EFFECTS - HANDS-ON
================================================================================
PART 1: OVERVIEW OF THE LESSON
================================================================================

WHAT YOU'LL LEARN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This lesson focuses on using the dependency array in useEffect to control when
an Effect needs to synchronize with an external system.

You will understand:

• What a dependency array is

• Why dependencies are important

• Effect behavior with no dependency array

• Effect behavior with an empty dependency array

• Effect behavior with one dependency

• Effect behavior with multiple dependencies

• How React compares dependencies

• How state and props become dependencies

• How cleanup works when dependencies change

• Creating an adjustable timer

• Fetching data when search values change

• Debouncing search requests

• Cancelling obsolete requests

• Avoiding unnecessary Effect executions

• Avoiding dependency-related loops


================================================================================
PART 2: WHAT IS A DEPENDENCY ARRAY?
================================================================================

A dependency array is the second argument passed to useEffect.

Basic syntax:

useEffect(() => {

  // Effect setup

}, [
  dependencies
])


Example:

useEffect(() => {

  console.log(
    searchTerm
  )

}, [
  searchTerm
])


Here:

searchTerm


is a dependency.


================================================================================
WHAT DOES THE DEPENDENCY ARRAY DO?
================================================================================

The dependency array describes the reactive values used by the Effect.

Conceptually:

Effect Uses Value
      ↓
Value Included as Dependency
      ↓
Value Changes
      ↓
Previous Cleanup
      ↓
Effect Synchronizes Again


Example:

searchTerm:

'java'


changes to:

'spring'


Effect:

Runs again using:

'spring'


================================================================================
IMPORTANT MENTAL MODEL
================================================================================

Avoid thinking:

"The dependency array is just a manual switch telling React when I want the
Effect to run."


Better:

THE DEPENDENCY ARRAY DESCRIBES
THE REACTIVE VALUES USED BY THE EFFECT.


If an Effect uses:

userId


then:

userId


normally belongs in the dependency array.


================================================================================
PART 3: THE THREE DEPENDENCY ARRAY PATTERNS
================================================================================

+----------------------+-------------------------------+------------------------+
| PATTERN              | BEHAVIOR                      | COMMON USE             |
+----------------------+-------------------------------+------------------------+
| No array             | After every relevant          | Synchronization needed |
|                      | committed render              | after every render     |
|                      |                               |                        |
| []                   | No changing reactive          | Initial setup/resource |
|                      | dependencies                  | synchronization        |
|                      |                               |                        |
| [value]              | Mount + value changes         | Resource depends on    |
|                      |                               | value                  |
|                      |                               |                        |
| [a, b]               | Mount + a or b changes        | Resource depends on    |
|                      |                               | multiple values        |
+----------------------+-------------------------------+------------------------+


================================================================================
PATTERN 1: NO DEPENDENCY ARRAY
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  console.log(
    'Effect executed'
  )

})


Conceptually:

Render
 ↓
Effect


Render Again
 ↓
Effect Again


IMPORTANT:

This is NOT automatically incorrect.


But if the Effect performs:

fetch()

setState()

subscriptions

or expensive synchronization


after every render, it may be unnecessary or may create a loop.


================================================================================
PATTERN 2: EMPTY DEPENDENCY ARRAY
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  connect()


  return () => {

    disconnect()
  }

}, [])


Conceptually:

The Effect does not depend on changing reactive values.


================================================================================
IMPORTANT [] CORRECTION
================================================================================

Do NOT memorize:

[] =
GUARANTEED EXACTLY ONCE


A better mental model is:

[] =
NO CHANGING REACTIVE DEPENDENCIES


In development, React Strict Mode can intentionally perform:

Setup
 ↓
Cleanup
 ↓
Setup


to help detect incorrect Effects.


================================================================================
PATTERN 3: VALUES IN DEPENDENCY ARRAY
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  fetchUser(
    userId
  )

}, [
  userId
])


Effect depends on:

userId


When:

userId changes


React performs the synchronization using the new value.


================================================================================
PART 4: THE PROBLEM WE'RE SOLVING
================================================================================

Suppose the application searches for books.

State:

searchTerm


The backend request depends on:

searchTerm


Flow:

User Changes Search
        ↓
searchTerm Changes
        ↓
Effect Dependency Changes
        ↓
Search Effect Runs
        ↓
Backend Request
        ↓
New Results


================================================================================
COMPARISON TABLE
================================================================================

+--------------------------+---------------------------+-------------------------+
| SCENARIO                 | POORLY CONTROLLED EFFECT  | DEPENDENCY-DRIVEN       |
+--------------------------+---------------------------+-------------------------+
| Search value unchanged   | May fetch unnecessarily   | No new search Effect    |
|                          |                           |                         |
| Search value changes     | Fetch timing unclear      | Effect responds         |
|                          |                           |                         |
| Component renders for    | May request again         | No request unless       |
| unrelated reason         |                           | dependency changed      |
|                          |                           |                         |
| Resource synchronization | Harder to reason about    | Linked to reactive data |
+--------------------------+---------------------------+-------------------------+


================================================================================
IMPORTANT SEARCH CLARIFICATION
================================================================================

If:

searchTerm


is a dependency, then the Effect runs whenever:

searchTerm changes.


If searchTerm changes on every keystroke:

J
 ↓
Ja
 ↓
Jav
 ↓
Java


then the Effect can still respond to every keystroke.


The dependency array alone does NOT debounce the search.


To avoid making a request after every keystroke, use:

DEBOUNCING


which we cover later in this lesson.


