PART 6: HANDS-ON - ADJUSTABLE TIMER
================================================================================

This example clearly demonstrates how dependency changes trigger:

CLEANUP
+
NEW SETUP


================================================================================
IMPORTANT NAMING CORRECTION
================================================================================

Avoid:

const [
  interval,
  setInterval
] = useState(1000)


Why?

The name:

setInterval


conflicts conceptually with the browser's:

setInterval()


function.


Use:

intervalMs

setIntervalMs


instead.


================================================================================
COMPLETE AdjustableTimer.jsx
================================================================================

import {
  useEffect,
  useState
} from 'react'


export default function AdjustableTimer() {

  // ============================================
  // STATE
  // ============================================

  const [
    count,
    setCount
  ] = useState(0)


  const [
    intervalMs,
    setIntervalMs
  ] = useState(1000)


  // ============================================
  // TIMER EFFECT
  // ============================================

  useEffect(() => {

    if (
      intervalMs <= 0
    ) {

      return
    }


    const timerId =
      window.setInterval(
        () => {

          setCount(
            previousCount =>
              previousCount + 1
          )

        },
        intervalMs
      )


    // ==========================================
    // CLEANUP
    // ==========================================

    return () => {

      window.clearInterval(
        timerId
      )

    }

  }, [
    intervalMs
  ])


  // ============================================
  // RENDER
  // ============================================

  return (
    <section>

      <h1>

        Timer:
        {' '}
        {count}

      </h1>


      <label htmlFor="interval">

        Interval (ms):

      </label>


      <input
        id="interval"
        type="number"
        min="100"
        step="100"
        value={
          intervalMs
        }
        onChange={
          event =>
            setIntervalMs(
              Number(
                event.target.value
              )
            )
        }
      />


      <p>

        Current interval:
        {' '}
        {intervalMs}ms

      </p>

    </section>
  )
}


================================================================================
CODE BREAKDOWN
================================================================================

+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
| CODE                       | PURPOSE                                   |
+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
| count                      | Number of timer ticks                     |
| setCount                   | Updates timer count                       |
| intervalMs                 | Current timer delay                       |
| setIntervalMs              | Updates delay                            |
| useEffect                  | Synchronizes timer                       |
| [intervalMs]               | Timer depends on interval value           |
| window.setInterval()       | Starts repeating timer                   |
| previousCount => + 1       | Updates from previous count              |
| clearInterval(timerId)     | Stops previous timer                     |
+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------+


================================================================================
WHY count IS NOT A DEPENDENCY
================================================================================

Inside the interval callback:

setCount(
  previousCount =>
    previousCount + 1
)


We use a functional state update.


The callback does NOT need to read:

count


directly.


Therefore the Effect doesn't need:

count


as a dependency.


This avoids resetting the timer on every count change.


================================================================================
BAD TIMER PATTERN
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const timerId =
    setInterval(() => {

      setCount(
        count + 1
      )

    }, intervalMs)


  return () =>
    clearInterval(
      timerId
    )

}, [
  intervalMs,
  count
])


Problem:

Every count update changes:

count


which causes:

Cleanup
 ↓
New Timer
 ↓
Count Changes
 ↓
Cleanup
 ↓
New Timer


The timer is unnecessarily recreated on every tick.


================================================================================
BETTER TIMER PATTERN
================================================================================

setCount(
  previous =>
    previous + 1
)


Dependencies:

[
  intervalMs
]


The Effect depends only on the value controlling the timer setup.


================================================================================
PART 7: HOW THE DEPENDENCY CHANGE WORKS
================================================================================

Initial:

intervalMs:

1000


Effect setup:

setInterval(..., 1000)


Timer:

Tick every 1 second


================================================================================
USER CHANGES TO 2000
================================================================================

State:

intervalMs:

1000
 ↓
2000


React renders using:

2000


Then:

Previous Effect Cleanup
      ↓
clearInterval(oldTimer)
      ↓
New Effect Setup
      ↓
setInterval(..., 2000)


Now:

Timer ticks every 2 seconds.


================================================================================
FLOW
================================================================================

intervalMs = 1000
       ↓
Effect Setup
       ↓
Timer A = 1000ms
       ↓
User Changes intervalMs
       ↓
intervalMs = 2000
       ↓
Render
       ↓
Cleanup Timer A
       ↓
Effect Setup
       ↓
Timer B = 2000ms


