PART 15: DEPENDENCY COMPARISON
================================================================================

React compares dependency values between renders.


Conceptually:

Previous dependency

vs

Current dependency


If values differ according to React's dependency comparison:

Effect needs to synchronize again.


================================================================================
PRIMITIVE EXAMPLE
================================================================================

Previous:

searchTerm = 'java'


Current:

searchTerm = 'spring'


Different:

YES


Effect re-synchronizes.


================================================================================
OBJECT REFERENCE EXAMPLE
================================================================================

Be careful with objects created during render.


Example:

const filters = {

  category,

  sortBy

}


useEffect(() => {

  fetchBooks(
    filters
  )

}, [
  filters
])


Each render can create a new object reference.


Even if the contents look the same:

Previous Object
 !==
New Object


Effect may therefore run more often than intended.


================================================================================
BETTER
================================================================================

useEffect(() => {

  const filters = {

    category,

    sortBy

  }


  fetchBooks(
    filters
  )

}, [
  category,
  sortBy
])


Dependencies now describe the real reactive inputs.


================================================================================
PART 16: DON'T REMOVE DEPENDENCIES JUST TO STOP AN EFFECT
================================================================================

Suppose:

useEffect(() => {

  fetchUser(
    userId
  )

}, [
  userId
])


You notice it runs whenever:

userId changes.


Do NOT simply change it to:

useEffect(() => {

  fetchUser(
    userId
  )

}, [])


just to stop reruns.


Now the Effect uses:

userId


but does not describe it as a dependency.


That can result in stale synchronization.


================================================================================
GOOD QUESTION
================================================================================

Ask:

WHY DOES THIS EFFECT RUN?


Then inspect:

• What reactive values does it read?

• Does it update state?

• Does that state change its dependencies?

• Are objects/functions recreated during render?

• Is the Effect actually needed?


